  technological change have been dominated  much more by theory where the theory  just builds up on on highly mathematical  constructs based on very very strong  assumptions and it kinds a it kind of it  kinds of produces more and more  publications without you know shedding  light on what's truly going on in  technological change so let me tell you  what one of the concerns I have with the  field of economics at least in most of  American colleges and universities and  you tell me number one if you agree or  disagree that this is an issue and  ideally whether you think it's connected  to what you just outlined which is I am  very concerned about the percentage of  the time that is spent teaching sort of  rational actor traditional economics  relative to the amount of time that's  spent really exploring the last twenty  years of behavioral economics and sort  of the way that a lot of these concepts  actually manifest in the real world do  you agree that that's a problem and is  that related to the problem you've  identified yes it is I agree it's a  problem and I and I agree that it's  related I think what's happened in  economics and part of it might be  tribute about to work by Gary Becker and  others has been this kind of option that  we don't have to truly understand human  behavior if we have an interesting model  that we can study and we could study the  mathematical properties of that model  and impress each other with what we've  discovered in our mathematical models so  it's sort of like understanding human  behavior is an option and there's a  substitute  which is you know more high-powered  theory where we just you know rediscover  essentially our assumptions about it and  it's amazing how this has affected so  many things because it also affects  economic policy because then you know if  you if you have an an economic problem  and we're trying to solve it and a  distinguished professor has a certain  theory based primarily on assumptions  but then can say I published a proof  that this is the best solution it's real  with the profession it doesn't have a  capability to really challenge that so  there isn't a strong enough leadership  to say wait a minute  how much of these assumptions are  grounded in reality you know the  assumptions are published in them in an  impressive journal then that sort of  like automatically elevates it to  acceptance do you believe that sort of  classical rational actor economics is  still a good basis for s students of  economics to first sort of understand if  humans were totally rational if we had  completely free markets if the laws of  supply and demand applied 100 percent in  the real world here's sort of how it  would work and then later be introduced  to for example the teachings of  behavioral economics or the limits of  capitalism or whatever else is that's  still the right order in your mind or  would you teach economics in a different  order I think it the the simple basis of  the ZURB neoclassical framework could  still be taught at the very first level  of explaining what supply and demand is  and maximizing utility and what  determines prices and everything else so  I think there's still that basic you  know element but then you know rather  than but then soon thereafter we've got  to introduce all the caveats and all the  other things that determine human  behavior before going you know  before piling it higher and deeper into  our own reality I think so maybe I don't  I don't know if you can speak to this  but I think that so my undergraduate  economics degree is from the University  of Massachusetts in Amherst  Massachusetts and I believe that that  was a unique Department insofar as you  know I had Marxist economist Richard  Wolff teaching my introduction to  macroeconomics a hundred level class as  a first year student and then very  quickly I was able to move into you know  the late Steve Resnick's class on  Marxian economics and other classes that  explored sort of the limits of  neoclassical economics was that  relatively unusual as far as economics  departments go I think so I think that  is very unusual you know I'm aware of  certain that you know departments like  that and some still exist you know but  that is unusual but we have to we have  to also be cautious because I think when  we're talking about I guess what we're  saying here is you're talking about  pluralism and economics and different  you know views other than the sort of  the neoclassical framework one thing  that that I talk about my book is we can  get too caught up in those scholarly  discussions and like you could say well  well this kind of helps round out  economics so that we understand things  better that's true but then you could  also you know we create a lot of other  distortions because you can have  scholars in the room talking about you  know what did Marx mean when he said  this that and the other thing and and  that that wouldn't be very productive  for economics they either so just the  general you know challenging the  simplicity of the new classical  framework is very good and principles  basic principles like labor exploitation  and those kind of things are good too  but if we get too much into the  and to scholarly discussions than a lot  of professors in those subfields tend to  have the same problem trying to impress  each other not necessarily with  mathematics but with you know their  quotations of what scholars had written  hundreds of years ago that kind of thing  do you think that so again anecdotally  when I was at the University of  Massachusetts economics was not part of  the business school it was it was part  of social sciences or of Humanities call  it what you want it has since been moved  into the business school and this is  something that we're actually seeing  around the country that increasingly  economics is being put under the  umbrella of learning about business is  that something that makes sense or  should we be concerned about that I I  would be concerned about that you know  you have to take these things into  context so if they're if it's a small  university they simply may not have an  economics department right and they may  teach a few economics classes and and it  would go under the business school but  like when I study economics at Columbia  was in the Graduate School of Arts and  Sciences and we separate from the  business school and I think that's right  because they think economics you know  really should be seen as as a science as  a social science with applications to  business without also applications to  public policy applications international  trade you know that kind of thing how  concerned are you about sort of pop  economics I guess for lack of a better  term that's leading to just a lot of  people believing that they understand  economics in a way that they can  accurately assess what sort of public  and government policies would be  beneficial to the economy is that having  a real impact on government or is that  something that's just sort of going on  in media and is not impacting public  policy um I think it's always impacted  public policy and it's gotten worse in  some ways because what's happened and  maybe there might be some trends you  know to improve things recently but it's  hard to say but what's what's happened  is that  have become so complicated and so  dependent on very specific assumptions  that you can approach them with with  alternative sets of assumptions that  essentially rediscover those assumptions  and make very different conclusions so  you know if you wanna you know if you  want a model that says that if we reduce  the tax rate the economy will grow you  can produce a model like that if you  want a model that says the opposite you  could produce a model like that and and  you know the the devil is in the details  of the assumptions and the math and and  that kind of thing and even the way data  are process them and measured and  interpreted so what that what that has  meant for public policy is that there's  a lot of distrust and perhaps rightfully  so in the applications of economic  models in general and so that really  limits our ability to use economics  because if we don't trust economics then  we're just basing policies on God  instinct or what we really want to  believe if we don't like taxes we'll  think it faxes are bad if we want public  programs you think taxes is good to  finance our programs and so it's kind of  like economics has become so theoretical  that it's it's created a disbelief in  economics and so a lot of public leaders  simply don't believe economics we've  been speaking even pacing the book is  how economics professors can stop  failing us the discipline at a  crossroads very interesting topic and  and I find it particularly important  right now Steven thanks so much for  talking to us about this can I just I  just want to show this because the book  is on sale half-price oh sure ah and  it's a coupon at this website  absolutely fantastic ere leap org slash  P dot PDF for a radio and podcast  audience Steven thank you so much oh  thank you David thank you very much

